# Average length of original papers: Experimental Economics vs Psychology

**Data:** `data/meta-regression_study_data_heterogeneity_in_replication_projects.csv` (927 rows x 57 cols).
**Unit of analysis:** original study (`drop_duplicates('id')` -> 141 studies), because ml1/ml3 rows are
lab-level replications (16 ids x 37 rows, 10 ids x 22 rows, incl. 26 `aggregated=True` rows).
`length` (pages of the ORIGINAL paper) is `int64`, 0 missing, range 1-45, and constant within every `id`.
**No join** with `meta-regression_replication_success_...csv` (143 rows x 15 cols; covers EERP/SSRP incl.
Experimental Philosophy & Social Sciences; contains NO length/pages column).

## Main table (study level, no `drop` filter)
| grouping | role | n | mean | bootstrap CI95 | median | SD | min-max |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Experimental Economics (`project=='ee'`) | MAIN | 18 | **28.00** | [24.83, 31.28] | 27.5 | 7.37 | 16-45 |
| Psychology (`project=='rpp'`) | MAIN | 97 | **11.22** | [10.01, 12.43] | 9.0 | 6.30 | 1-31 |
| Psychology alt A (`rpp+ml1+ml3`) | alternative | 123 | 11.07 | [9.85, 12.21] | 9.0 | 6.45 | 1-31 |
| Psychology alt B (`discipline` Social+Cognitive) | alternative | 123 | 11.07 | [10.00, 12.24] | 9.0 | 6.45 | 1-31 |

ee == `discipline=='Economics'` (18/18), no overlap with any other project.
Bootstrap: 2000 resamples, percentile 2.5-97.5, seed 20261022. The ee interval is based on n=18 only.

## Comparison ee vs rpp
| quantity | value | bootstrap CI95 |
|---|---|---|
| mean ee - mean rpp (pages) | 16.78 | [13.46, 20.43] |
| mean ee / mean rpp (ratio) | 2.50 | [2.12, 2.92] |

Cross-checks: bootstrap two-sided p for difference > 0 = 0.0000; Welch t = 9.06 (df 21.9, p = 7.4e-09);
Mann-Whitney U = 1681.5 (p = 4.5e-10), P(length_ee > length_rpp) = 0.963.
Every ee paper (min 16 pp) is longer than the rpp median (9 pp); 27/97 rpp papers fall inside the ee range.

## Sensitivity controls (none changes the conclusion)
| control | n | mean | note |
|---|---|---|---|
| rpp main | 97 | 11.22 | |
| rpp excluding `drop==True` | 90 | 11.00 | 7 flagged studies |
| rpp row-wise (927 rows, no dedup) | 97 | 11.22 | identical: labs are balanced per study |
| rpp trimmed 5%/tail | 87 | 10.95 | |
| rpp winsorized at p95 | 97 | 11.03 | |
| ee main | 18 | 28.00 | |
| ee excluding `drop==True` | 18 | 28.00 | no ee study flagged |
| ee row-wise (no dedup) | 18 | 28.00 | |
| ee trimmed 5%/tail | 16 | 27.69 | |
| ee winsorized at p95 | 18 | 27.86 | |

Short papers (1-3 pages) kept as valid data: 8/97 rpp (8.2%), 2/16 ml1 (12.5%), 0/18 ee.
Excluding all of them would move rpp from 11.22 to 12.00 and widen the gap, so the main estimate is conservative.

## Caveats
- Scope is limited to these replication-project samples (18 ee, 97 rpp original papers); it is not a census of
  all economics vs psychology publishing.
- `length` coding (printed vs online-first pages) is not documented in the metadata.
- The gap partly reflects venue conventions (ee papers are AER/QJE articles, 16-45 pp) rather than only field-wide norms.
